envelope: 1
uuid: nexus-export-schema-response
type: schema
id: response
context: lib/node-schemas — shared node-type schema registry
intent: null
summary: null
system: null
tags: []
exported_at: 1789330000000
source: nexus.lib.node-export
payload:
  status: REAL
  source: >-
    §MCO16e 2026-09-13, James's own correction: "`.response` would need to be a node type in the
    taxonomy... then used for the dom mutation listener." Genuinely distinct from `.output`
    (schema.output) — checked directly, not assumed: `.output` is a captured, TERMINAL answer to
    one specific request, read once after completion. A `.response` is one of many LIVE, per-event
    records emitted while a compartment is still open — no terminal state, no single jobId to key
    on. Grounded in two real writers: lib/compartment-dom-ledger.js's real recordDomEvent() (the
    compartment_dom_ledger table, §MCO10) and guardian/server.js's new real POST /dom/event
    (§MCO16e) — both accept and persist exactly this shape.
  fields:
    uuid:
      type: string
      required: true
      description: real, unique id for this response event — generated at insert time, never reused
    compartmentUuid:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: >-
        the real, live compartment this response belongs to — dropped, not fabricated (§1.2, same
        rule recordDomEvent() already enforces). §MCO16f 2026-09-14 — relaxed from required:true
        when jobId was added: a job-origin DOM event genuinely has no compartment, and
        node-schemas.js's checkPayload() cannot express "one of these two" (see NODE-TAXONOMY.md's
        own "Still mechanically open" note). The real constraint — at least one of
        compartmentUuid/jobId — is enforced in guardian/lib/response-nodes.js's
        writeResponseNode(), which is where it can actually be checked.
    jobId:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: >-
        §MCO16f 2026-09-14 — the real guardian job this DOM event belongs to, when the event came
        from a job rather than a compartment. Added for guardian/lib/response-nodes.js, the real
        second writer of this type (userscript startWatch, which has a jobId and no compartment).
    sequence:
      type: number
      required: false
      description: >-
        monotonic index of this event within one job/compartment — real ordering that survives
        equal-millisecond timestamps, which a live MutationObserver genuinely produces
    queueId:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: the real RAID queue row this compartment is executing, when one exists — honestly null otherwise, never guessed
    domEventType:
      type: string
      required: true
      description: 'the real event kind — e.g. dom.mutation, dom.snapshot, dom.ready'
    agentId:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: which real agent/provider produced this event, when known
    payload:
      type: object
      required: false
      description: the real, event-specific data — DOM diff/snapshot content, never fabricated when absent
    ts:
      type: number
      required: true
      description: real event timestamp — logical ordering axis for a stream of these (§3.2), not wall-clock display metadata
